[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug: the parser reads dates month first. Read the reports
by tenant, form and what the reporter describes, and #4471 holds three problems. Each now has a
ticket of its own. The counts below are computed from the export at render time, the way the
desk's own cause table counts them.

<SplitTable ticket={4471} repo="junners/mega.dev-s01e03-what-it-shows" />

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed ("parse dd/mm for eu locales"), and the
booking-v3 hint is won't fix because the booking-v3 form is retired. The one-day moves are in
neither: that ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## 1 · The parser reads day-first dates month first

Every tenant here writes the day first. The shared parser reads month first, so day and month
swap: "7 April went out on 4 July". This is the bug #4471 names. **Fixed** on 5 October:
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

## 2 · booking-v3 shows a month-first hint

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People do what the hint says and type the month
first, then the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The swap looks the same as
problem 1, so the subject lines match and decision 0001 merged it. It only comes in through the
form, only for the booking-v3 tenants. The parser fix does not touch it. **Won't fix**: the
booking-v3 form is retired, so the wrong hint goes with it.

## 3 · Invites one day off

These dates do not swap: they move by one day. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto
got Monday." The desk read these as one-day moves (desk notes) but filed them under the parser by
tenant. They stay counted there, as the desk counts them, and they have a ticket of their own. A
one-day move with the right day and month points at time zones, not date order. **Open**: the
engineering update that closed #4471 does not cover it, and neither fix changes it.

## Why one ticket hid three

- Decision 0001 merges on subject line. All three problems read "wrong day" in the subject.
- Intake tags by the reporter's browser locale. The cause depends on the tenant's locale and form,
  so the tags in the table disagree with the problems in both directions.
- The export pages overlap: some reports come twice. Count by id, once.

